{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for 992b2b65 (S2b foreign producer provenance), on preparation 21b4924cb/e832f1a2d.\n\nAdopted: the fixture configuration route, with these fixed points.\n\n1. Identity. The producer of a retained foreign copy is the exact ScopeRef (scope, incarnation, kind) carried in the copy's own canonical bytes, read from its header with the same bounded metadata query the local prefix uses. The `under` name, a receiver alias, a digest in an allowlist, a fixture tag, or a caller-supplied owner table is never identity and never coverage.\n\n2. Pin map. The map from ScopeRef to pin (definition digest or platform name) and declaration metadata is an immutable input to the pass, supplied through the owner's configuration port before the inventory frame opens, bounded by a cited count, and built in the fixture from the actual native births (genesis 0) of the installed graph read through the existing reader. It is the fixture form of the installed-graph resolver that decision 89376d74 point 4 allows; the port is labelled fixture in its type name and at the call site. No persisted provenance at retention time: that is a store and runtime change outside S2b, and the directory is already the authority for which scopes a room contains. Record it under the implementation note's open questions for A0b, not as S2b work.\n\n3. Policy. The ReviewedArchivePolicy registry stays a separate table keyed by pin, covering inline input classes, owners, records, steps and domains. For every direct `uses` reference the pass joins the copy header to its pin through the map and checks every selected local and foreign class and support policy before any copied, value, rule or snapshot body fetch. Missing ScopeRef, missing pin, a pin absent from the registry, or any conflict between the map and something verifiable (a retained genesis copy's own seed; the declaration's digest against retained definitions) refuses the whole prefix, fail closed, with a named reason. Body selection then verifies canonical bytes, raw content digest and the exact consumer FactRef the local entry names, as you describe.\n\n4. Fences. The marked selection records each foreign source's ScopeRef and the pin it was classified under; rebuild after eviction or restart verifies the same pair and refuses on a changed configuration. The pass manifest names each foreign source by ScopeRef and pin so a reader of the archive can tell the policy basis. Pass budgets, the one synchronous inventory transaction, and the final scope, pin and cutoff fences are unchanged. An archived foreign entry brings in nothing further (archive note 15, inputs table); no recursive foreign-history export.\n\n5. Scope. Native birth and setup are explicit trusted fixture preparation under the A2/A3 cutoff, not a public RPC, request policy, production certificate or consent. A0b stays out. The integrated witness exports an installed application's scopes including a late-bound genesis 0 and reads the result back with the existing reader; export and structural readback establish no production permission and no complete offline foreign-history verification. Reviewed G5a and S2a landings come before the final integrated gate, and S2b's head must state which main it is built on.\n\nProceed to the runtime implementation on this basis.","body":{"to":"builder"}}